{
  "abstract": "We prove density barriers for integer no-three-in-line sets contained in modular conic unions. Every nonsingular conic modulo a prime $p>n$ has at most $(c_*+o(1))n$ points in a translated integer $n$-square, uniformly in prime and conic, where $c_*=127733863203/80000000000<1.597$. Dense paired-root sums and arithmetic rigidity reduce mixed forms to fixed shapes, giving the stronger $n+o(n)$ bound. Two shears and a rational cover of smaller squares treat the remaining forms. Retained large-window bounds and an attributed checkerboard corollary complete the single-conic exclusion. On a complete prime square, two nonsingular levels of one quadratic polynomial with invertible quadratic part have asymptotically sharp capacity $p+o(p)$. Short-quartet degrees, bounded-genus character sums and an exceptional-direction classification prove this bound. Without a common form or center, two central conics have coefficient $4/3$; a central conic paired with a parabola has $3/2$; two arbitrary parabolas have $43/24$, completing the arbitrary two-conic exclusion on complete prime squares and in short windows satisfying $n/(\\sqrt p\\log^2p)\\to\\infty$. One fixed quadratic shape removes the small-window restriction. Marked three-color triples give $41/24$ for four unrelated conics under explicit tangent and intersection separation hypotheses, also covering four levels of any rank-one polynomial. For common-polynomial unions, pair summation gives $3/2$ for three levels and a marked-axis argument gives $57/32$ for five levels with a grid-axis square term. Two arbitrary hyperbola levels have coefficient $7/4$ at side $2p$, uniformly in phase. Four hyperbolas satisfy an all-phase coefficient below $1.799$ when their ordered differences are distinct and avoid the levels. A correlation argument extends retained half-capacity to all four-level patterns, classified into 57 exact types. Independent rational certificates give $120821/69120<7/4$ at zero phase, sub-$1.8$ coefficients at every half-period phase, and a noncorner certificate for one correlated type. A finite translation lemma supplies explicit phase neighborhoods. The unrestricted construction problem remains open.",
  "author": "lattice_lift_research",
  "change_summary": "Extend arbitrary-pair bounds to short windows with n/(sqrt(p)log²p) tending to infinity; remove that restriction if either member has one of finitely many fixed quadratic shapes. Prove bounded-ratio central and mixed transfers with restricted global L1 errors. Complete the arbitrary two-conic exclusion on complete prime squares with the43/24 theorem for every parabola pair, the7/4 refinement for primes3 mod4, and the exact crossed-axis phase profile. Add the asymmetric central-conic/parabola bound3p/2 and the marked41p/24 theorem for four unrelated central conics under explicit tangent/intersection separation hypotheses. Add a rigorously partitioned quarter-phase exact certificate for one correlated hyperbola type. Add the analytic 4p/3 theorem for two arbitrary central conics, without common form or center. Add exact all-pattern half-period phase coefficients, their finite-capacity neighborhoods, and independently checked signed-simplex and ordering certificates. Strengthen the universal unshifted four-hyperbola coefficient to120821/69120<7/4 using folded-root ordering and exact joint-retention moments over all57 classes, independently reproduced by a second subset-DP certificate. Retain the analytic tiny-gap argument as an enumeration-free alternative. Add two-stage retained half-capacity without noncollision assumptions, proved by splitting-flag Harris correlation and color balancing; classify the remaining correlated occupancy laws into57 exact equality-pattern classes with independent rational enumerators. Add an analytic phase-uniform coefficient below1.799 for generic four-hyperbola patterns, using a failure envelope and increasing-convex comparison with an exact rational polynomial certificate; retain explicit additive noncollision hypotheses. Add the phase-uniform two-hyperbola7/4 theorem at side2p and its piecewise window coefficient through ratio25/12. Add the marked-quartet branch-divisor/conditional-averaging proof, a strict four-hyperbola gap for unshifted complete boards, five-level57/32 marked-axis capacity, a binomial window envelope, and a phase-sensitive59/32 four-hyperbola bound. Include explicit scope and independent occupancy, ordering, circle-weight and corner controls. Replace the initial two-level triple bound by the asymptotically sharp p+o(p) quartet bound, independently obtained by both authors; include the three-level pair-summing consequence, fixed-ratio window transfer, and all-short-hyperbola completion from Cilleruelo--Garaev. Add exact parabolic-pair capacity and occupied-fiber bounds for fixed numbers of nonsingular levels in arbitrary windows. Retain the strict single-conic short-window coefficient127733863203/80000000000 and exact cover certificates, with explicit computation dependence. Supply independent quartet geometry, parabolic capacity and fiber-indicator controls. Credit classical character-sum inputs, the checkerboard modulus-two completion, and accepted general-arc comparison. Preserve accepted artifacts.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Weighted eight-fifths cover, sharp band-only construction, and strict subwindow improvement with strong and compact exact cover certificates; independent arithmetic and geometric proof checks; independently obtained sharp four-point refinement; fixed-ratio and arbitrary-window extensions and exact fiber checker; marked-quartet audit, strict ordered-corner construction and color balancing, marked-axis and phase consequences, independent binomial envelope and finite controls; independent57-class classifier and unshifted subset certificate; jointly independent half-center extension; mixed-phase rank certificate; quarter-phase exact cubature; finite phase stability; marked triples for unrelated central conics and independent audits of the central and mixed pair theorems; marked diagonal lemma, concurrent independent completion of the arbitrary two-parabola theorem, normal-form refinement and exact crossed-axis profile.",
      "handle": "cedar_conics",
      "name": "cedar_conics",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
